{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794424742,"nonce":"qdF0rz118UiG6Quv/MnxO9X4rTs=","message":"Numbers beyond `i32` and `f64` landed in 8248a85.\n\n- `i8`, `i16`, `u8`, `u16`, `isize`, `usize`: a Haxe `Int`. Going in, `T::try_from(value)`; coming back, `i32::from` or, for the two sizes, `i32::try_from`. A value that does not fit is `Fault::Range`, status -11, \"number out of range\", raised before the function runs or after it returned.\n- `u32`: the same 32 bits, `as` both ways, and `UInt` in the Haxe signature. The natives still declare `Int`.\n- `f32`: a Haxe `Float`, narrowed with `as` and widened with `f64::from`.\n- The wire, the C header, the HashLink signatures and the glue do not change: the model keeps `Type::I32` or `Type::F64` and notes the Rust number beside it (`Param::number`, `Method::returns_number`). The manifest names the Rust type.\n- Only as a parameter or a result, plain or in a `Result`. Not in an `Option`, not what a future, stream or signal carries, not in a lent function's signature.\n\nWeighed and not done: `i64`, `u64` and wider are refused, with a message saying to use an `f64` or split the value. Crossing them properly takes a 64-bit wire type and `haxe.Int64` on the Haxe side (`hl.I64`, `cpp.Int64`), which is a change to the ABI and not a conversion. Checked narrowing to `Int` was rejected for them: a timestamp would fail at run time on most values.\n\nRun: tests/counter/run.sh in all eight modes on macOS, gen-hl and hl three more times in each Ash mode, hl and gen-hl on stock HashLink; tests/examples/run.sh on Ash, Ash wasm, hxcpp and Emscripten. The fixture's `Numbers` interface gives each kind back and is checked for both ends of each range.","files":null}]}